ref 1062 #uuid "9b35013c-5ff4-5a7c-99fa-9b6c887c8c75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111591 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111592 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111592 #edb/ref 1061 3553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111592 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8fb38c77-55f0-5928-8c35-70c3ff0de790" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111592 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111593 #edb/ref 1061 3556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111593 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d9b9bca6-86d4-5640-8cb5-7659bf64aa00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111593 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111823 #edb/ref 1064 "The **power of quotient rule** states that if we want to raise the quotient $\\dfrac{x}{y}$ to some power $n,$ we must raise the numerator and denominator to the power $n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^n=\\dfrac{x^n}{y^n}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^4=\\dfrac{x^4}{y^4}.\n$$\n\nTo see why the power of quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again the long way. We get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y} & = \\\\ \\frac{x\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x}{y\\cdot y\\cdot y\\cdot y} & = \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power of quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111823 #edb/ref 1065 1484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111823 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9aedc7af-544c-5314-a986-2cd6ce64479b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111823 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
